{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"5d67e9262d57b9e8","name":"card_event_the_tide_turns","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Japan/NavalWarfare/events/card_event_the_tide_turns","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"THE TIDE TURNS","text":"Shuffle your hand into your deck, draw equal amount. Deal damage to all units equal to Navy cards shuffled in.","flavorText":"After Japan's defeat at Midway in June 1942, the Imperial Navy's momentum in the Pacific collapsed. Four fleet carriers were lost in a single day along with hundreds of elite pilots. This disaster forced Japan to abandon its plan for a quick, decisive victory. Before Midway, naval doctrine focused on a massive showdown with the US fleet, named Kantai Kessen (English: naval fleet decisive battle). Instead, Midway turned into a trap. American codebreakers had discovered the Japanese plan and set up an ambush near Midway Atoll. Following this defeat, Japan shifted to a defensive strategy. It built new carriers like Taihō and focused on extending its island defenses. But its industrial base could not match the speed and scale of American shipbuilding. Even more critical was the loss of veteran pilots. Japan tried to speed up training programs, but the new crews lacked the skill and experience of those lost at Midway. From this point, Japan’s naval operations were mostly reactive. Midway did not just stop Japan's advance, it marked the moment when the tide of the Pacific War truly turned.","faction":"Japan","type":"order","rarity":"Unique","cost":3,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_the_tide_turns","title":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_the_tide_turns_title","SourceString":"THE TIDE TURNS","LocalizedString":"THE TIDE TURNS"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Japan","kredits":3,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_the_tide_turns_text","SourceString":"Shuffle your hand into your deck, draw equal amount. Deal damage to all units equal to Navy cards shuffled in.","LocalizedString":"Shuffle your hand into your deck, draw equal amount. Deal damage to all units equal to Navy cards shuffled in."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_the_tide_turns_history","SourceString":"After Japan's defeat at Midway in June 1942, the Imperial Navy's momentum in the Pacific collapsed. Four fleet carriers were lost in a single day along with hundreds of elite pilots. This disaster forced Japan to abandon its plan for a quick, decisive victory. Before Midway, naval doctrine focused on a massive showdown with the US fleet, named Kantai Kessen (English: naval fleet decisive battle). Instead, Midway turned into a trap. American codebreakers had discovered the Japanese plan and set up an ambush near Midway Atoll. Following this defeat, Japan shifted to a defensive strategy. It built new carriers like Taihō and focused on extending its island defenses. But its industrial base could not match the speed and scale of American shipbuilding. Even more critical was the loss of veteran pilots. Japan tried to speed up training programs, but the new crews lacked the skill and experience of those lost at Midway. From this point, Japan’s naval operations were mostly reactive. Midway did not just stop Japan's advance, it marked the moment when the tide of the Pacific War truly turned.","LocalizedString":"After Japan's defeat at Midway in June 1942, the Imperial Navy's momentum in the Pacific collapsed. Four fleet carriers were lost in a single day along with hundreds of elite pilots. This disaster forced Japan to abandon its plan for a quick, decisive victory. Before Midway, naval doctrine focused on a massive showdown with the US fleet, named Kantai Kessen (English: naval fleet decisive battle). Instead, Midway turned into a trap. American codebreakers had discovered the Japanese plan and set up an ambush near Midway Atoll. Following this defeat, Japan shifted to a defensive strategy. It built new carriers like Taihō and focused on extending its island defenses. But its industrial base could not match the speed and scale of American shipbuilding. Even more critical was the loss of veteran pilots. Japan tried to speed up training programs, but the new crews lacked the skill and experience of those lost at Midway. From this point, Japan’s naval operations were mostly reactive. Midway did not just stop Japan's advance, it marked the moment when the tide of the Pacific War truly turned."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Unique","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardPlayedFromHand"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Sleeping_Dragon_Drums_1'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Sleeping_Dragon_Drums_1.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Japan/NavalWarfare/t_the_tide_turns.t_the_tide_turns","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Tom Freeman","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/THE_TIDE_TURNS.THE_TIDE_TURNS","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":403.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_the_tide_turns_photo","SourceString":"Japanese heavy cruiser Mikuma sinking after the Battle of Midway, June 1942.","LocalizedString":"Japanese heavy cruiser Mikuma sinking after the Battle of Midway, June 1942."},"isDraw":true,"isDefenseBuff":true},"variables":[{"name":"navyCount","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"count","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"cardsToDamage","type":"TArray<int32>","direction":"in"},{"name":"cardIDS","type":"TArray<int32>","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_event_the_tide_turns","owner":"card_event_the_tide_turns_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final | FUNC_UbergraphFunction | FUNC_HasDefaults","loc